1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,350
The Curiosity Club.

2
00:00:02,350 --> 00:00:04,510
Episode 19.

3
00:00:04,510 --> 00:00:07,269
The Sandwich That Would Not Stay.

4
00:00:07,269 --> 00:00:09,986
Here is something to listen for.

5
00:00:09,986 --> 00:00:13,899
Has Earth's gravity disappeared where astronauts orbit?

6
00:00:13,899 --> 00:00:15,326
Ready?

7
00:00:15,326 --> 00:00:17,404
Let us find out.

8
00:00:17,404 --> 00:00:21,902
In a space video, an astronaut releases a small object.

9
00:00:21,902 --> 00:00:25,869
It floats beside her instead of dropping onto a table.

10
00:00:25,869 --> 00:00:29,062
My lunch would escape, Leo says.

11
00:00:29,062 --> 00:00:32,010
I would spend every meal chasing it.

12
00:00:32,010 --> 00:00:35,937
Pip imagines a sandwich wearing a tiny seat belt.

13
00:00:35,937 --> 00:00:38,722
Maya asks a bigger question.

14
00:00:38,722 --> 00:00:41,236
Is there no gravity up there?

15
00:00:41,236 --> 00:00:47,785
Ms. Park explains that Earth's gravity still acts strongly at the height of an orbiting space station.

16
00:00:47,785 --> 00:00:52,459
The station and the objects inside it are falling around Earth together.

17
00:00:52,459 --> 00:00:54,592
Falling around Earth?

18
00:00:54,592 --> 00:00:56,603
Leo repeats.

19
00:00:56,603 --> 00:01:00,747
The station also has a great deal of sideways motion.

20
00:01:00,747 --> 00:01:03,654
Its path curves around the planet.

21
00:01:03,654 --> 00:01:09,999
The astronauts and their surroundings share that motion, so the astronauts appear to float inside.

22
00:01:09,999 --> 00:01:14,076
The children use an animation to follow the curved path.

23
00:01:14,076 --> 00:01:18,043
They do not try to reproduce it by jumping off anything.

24
00:01:18,043 --> 00:01:21,616
Maya notices straps and holders in the video.

25
00:01:21,616 --> 00:01:25,380
Everyday objects need ways to stay in place.

26
00:01:25,380 --> 00:01:28,763
An astronaut catches a floating package.

27
00:01:28,763 --> 00:01:31,888
Leo cheers as if she has scored a goal.

28
00:01:31,888 --> 00:01:35,652
The club designs an imaginary space lunch tray.

29
00:01:35,652 --> 00:01:38,736
Maya adds clips for containers.

30
00:01:38,736 --> 00:01:42,119
Leo adds a place to hold a drink package.

31
00:01:42,119 --> 00:01:45,774
Pip adds a button labeled Find Missing Sandwich.

32
00:01:45,774 --> 00:01:47,975
What does that button do?

33
00:01:47,975 --> 00:01:49,823
asks Maya.

34
00:01:49,823 --> 00:01:53,233
It says, Leo, check behind you.

35
00:01:53,233 --> 00:01:56,182
Leo turns around automatically.

36
00:01:56,182 --> 00:02:00,815
Pip is very pleased with his first successful space-lunch test.

37
00:02:00,815 --> 00:02:03,722
Now, a little more to discover.

38
00:02:03,722 --> 00:02:12,078
Astronauts in an orbiting station experience apparent weightlessness because they and the station are in continuous free fall around Earth.

39
00:02:12,078 --> 00:02:14,701
Gravity has not disappeared.

40
00:02:14,701 --> 00:02:20,883
This environment changes many ordinary tasks, including handling objects and eating.

41
00:02:20,883 --> 00:02:25,135
Securing equipment keeps useful things from drifting away.

42
00:02:25,135 --> 00:02:27,975
Let us hear the main idea again.

43
00:02:27,975 --> 00:02:32,133
The floating object did not show that gravity was absent.

44
00:02:32,133 --> 00:02:36,671
The station and everything inside were falling around Earth together.

45
00:02:36,671 --> 00:02:41,711
The club then imagined practical ways to keep a space lunch in place.

46
00:02:41,711 --> 00:02:44,687
Here are three useful expressions.

47
00:02:44,687 --> 00:02:47,812
You can listen, or say them with me.

48
00:02:47,812 --> 00:02:49,864
Why does it float?

49
00:02:49,864 --> 00:02:51,915
Why does it float?

50
00:02:51,915 --> 00:02:54,361
Gravity is still acting.

51
00:02:54,361 --> 00:02:56,807
Gravity is still acting.

52
00:02:56,807 --> 00:02:59,266
Let us keep it in place.

53
00:02:59,266 --> 00:03:01,725
Let us keep it in place.

54
00:03:01,725 --> 00:03:04,320
Remember our listening question?

55
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:08,233
Has Earth's gravity disappeared where astronauts orbit?

56
00:03:08,233 --> 00:03:10,366
Here is the answer.

57
00:03:10,366 --> 00:03:11,847
No.

58
00:03:11,847 --> 00:03:17,336
Their apparent weightlessness comes from falling around Earth together with their spacecraft.

59
00:03:17,336 --> 00:03:20,991
And here is a question just for your imagination.

60
00:03:20,991 --> 00:03:27,160
Which ordinary activity would be funniest or most difficult if loose objects drifted around you?

61
00:03:27,160 --> 00:03:30,271
There is more than one possible answer.

62
00:03:30,271 --> 00:03:33,519
Keep wondering, and see you next time.
